// A trait's DEFAULT method body is type-checked (body-coverage arc).
//
// The second hole the body-coverage ledger found, unchecked for the same reason as the first:
// nothing walked it. A default body is ordinary code that really runs — every implementor that
// omits the method inherits it — so an unchecked one could ship a fault that only fires for the
// implementors who declined to override.
//
// It is checked ONCE, against the trait's own contract: `self` is `dyn <Trait>`, so a default body
// may rely on exactly what every implementor guarantees — the trait's own methods — and no more.
// Checking it once per implementor instead would re-report one authoring mistake at every `impl`,
// and would still not check a trait nobody has implemented yet.
// expect: error E0007 at 17:16
// expect: exit 1
trait Greeter {
    fn name(): string
    fn greet(): string {
        return 42
    }
}
class En {
    fn new(): En { return En {} }
}
impl Greeter for En {
    fn name(): string { return "en" }
}
